WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.500
<v Instructor>Okay, so let's practice</v>

2
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.850
using Amazon ElastiCache.

3
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.800
So we're going to get started.

4
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.200
Here we have several engine recommendations.

5
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.020
We have Valkey, which is a replacement for Redis,

6
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.480
Memcached or Redis OSS.

7
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.533
So you can use Valkey.

8
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:14.700
It's the recommended way.

9
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.110
We can use Redis, we're gonna use Redis.

10
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.850
It's the exact same options.

11
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.800
And then for deployment option,

12
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.240
we have the serverless option

13
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.770
and we have the node-based cluster,

14
00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.870
but for us to understand exactly how things work,

15
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.020
we're going to select the node-based cluster.

16
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.090
So we can restore from a backup,

17
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.710
or we have easy create

18
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.440
to use recommended best practices configuration.

19
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.500
So for example, we have production type of configuration

20
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.660
or dev test or demo.

21
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.000
And it depends on your needs, of course,

22
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.240
or if you wanted to, you can configure everything

23
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.820
by having the cluster cache mode.

24
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.950
So here we're going to want to configure everything

25
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.230
just to view all the options.

26
00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.410
So the cluster mode is going to be disabled,

27
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.800
where we have only a single shard with one primary node

28
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.990
and up to five read replica.

29
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.000
But if you want to have multiple shards

30
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.060
across multiple servers, then you would use cluster mode.

31
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.250
All right, so we'll use it at disabled.

32
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.370
Then this one is going to be called DemoCluster.

33
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.100
The location is going to be on AWS Cloud,

34
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.340
but you have the option to also run ElastiCache on premises

35
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.220
using something called AWS Outpost.

36
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.020
Now, do we want multi-AZ or not?

37
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.170
This is very helpful for high availability and failover

38
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.330
in case of a primary node failover,

39
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.040
but for now we disable it

40
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.220
because this will just incur more cost if we did.

41
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.320
Do we want auto-failover, yes or no?

42
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.390
We'll leave it as enabled.

43
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.630
Now for cluster setting, you can specify the engine version,

44
00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.670
the port, the parameter groups,

45
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.020
and for node type, we're going

46
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.990
to use a micro type of instance.

47
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.298
And so we have t2 micro, t3 micro,

48
00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:50.250
and t4g micro in this example,

49
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.010
and I think t2 and t3 are in the free tier

50
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.020
so use one of these.

51
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.030
All right, so we have a t2 micro,

52
00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.680
and then we can have replicas,

53
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.880
which is very helpful when you have this kind of scaling

54
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.120
for, as we said, the cluster mode.

55
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.730
So we are going to just have it as zero right now

56
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.160
just for cost purposes,

57
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.370
and this is of course, when you have multi-AZ,

58
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:15.510
you should have more replicas.

59
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.740
So this makes sense.

60
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.310
So let's keep it as zero just for cost purposes.

61
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.830
Now I'm going to create a subnet group,

62
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.680
so my-first-subnet-group,

63
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.860
and this is going to indicate in ElastiCache,

64
00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.440
which subnets we can run the cache in.

65
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.760
So we can choose the VPC,

66
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.910
and then automatically subnets are auto selected,

67
00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.510
but we can specify them as well if you wanted to.

68
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.980
Now for AZ placements,

69
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.500
we can specify which replicas can go to each AZ,

70
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.320
but again, we're not running into multi-AZ mode right now,

71
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.180
so it doesn't really matter.

72
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.910
Now for next, do we want encryption at rest, yes or no?

73
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.800
In case you want encryption at rest,

74
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.480
you need to specify a key.

75
00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.420
And do you want encryption in transit to encrypt the data

76
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.730
between the client and the server?

77
00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.920
If you do enable encryption in transit,

78
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.990
then you get access control feature.

79
00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.940
So to choose who can access your cache.

80
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.390
So we have Redis AUTH in which we specify a password,

81
00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.600
an AUTH token to connect to our Redis cluster,

82
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.810
or we can use an user group access control list.

83
00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.120
And then you can create a user group right here

84
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.280
from the ElastiCache console.

85
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.470
So I'm going to disable encryption in transit.

86
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.930
And then you can also have security groups

87
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.620
to manage which applications have access

88
00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.470
to your cluster from a network perspective.

89
00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.460
So in terms of backup, do we want backup, yes or no?

90
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.920
And then for maintenance, do we want maintenance windows

91
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.340
to actually perform minor versions upgrades?

92
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.530
Do we want to have logs such as slow logs

93
00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.650
or engine logs that we can send into CloudWatch logs?

94
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.570
And finally, do we want to have tags?

95
00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.860
So a lot of different options.

96
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.270
This is similar to RDS,

97
00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.220
that's why I'm going a bit quicker over this.

98
00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.100
But we've seen the different options to create our cache.

99
00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.690
So then we can review everything

100
00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.933
and then click on create to actually create our cache.

101
00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.520
My ElastiCache database is now created

102
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.570
and I can click on it.

103
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.870
And if I had an application that I wrote with some code,

104
00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.270
I could actually use the primary endpoints

105
00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.790
or if it was a read replica, a reader endpoint,

106
00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.580
to allow to read from my cache, okay?

107
00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.400
Now, there is no easy way for me to show you how

108
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.300
to connect to an Redis cache because it's not that easy

109
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.100
and you need to actually write some code

110
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.720
and it would be too complicated.

111
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.900
But from an AWS perspective, from this console,

112
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.350
you can see all the details as well as nodes, metrics,

113
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.390
logs, network security, and so on.

114
00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.160
And it looks just like RDS

115
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.920
because yes, it is very similar service to RDS,

116
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.760
but it's intended for Redis and Memcached.

117
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.240
So the last thing you have to do is take this Redis cluster,

118
00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.890
action and then delete.

119
00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:50.970
And then do you wanna back up?

120
00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:51.803
No.

121
00:04:51.803 --> 00:04:54.870
You just type the name of the cluster, delete,

122
00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.610
and you're done with this hands on.

123
00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.443
Okay, that's it.

124
00:04:57.443 --> 00:05:00.340
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

